  "conclusion": "Adding a second, independent model family (gemini-2.5-flash-lite, via OpenRouter) does NOT resolve E4's degeneracy -- Acc@10% is exactly 0 for BOTH models independently (0/36 each) and for the 72-item pool. This upgrades the finding from 'one model's accuracy happened to saturate' to 'the 10%-tolerance decoupling test could not be run on either of two independently-built current-generation VLMs on this task' -- a stronger, more general version of the same null result, not a different one. The continuous-relative-error substitute correlation is negative for both models individually (Qwen r=-0.481, gemini r=-0.218) and pooled (r=-0.460, CI excludes 0), but per project_sgf_e4_results.md's original confound analysis this should still not be over-read as a clean coupling finding: gemini also shows a constrained output palette relative to the perturbation (calibration reads plateau at 0.5/1.0 mm/tick and never reach the true alpha=2 asserted value of 2.0 on either session; velocity does not move in a consistent direction under alpha, see e2_gemini_flash_lite_results.json), so the same 'both axes downstream of the same non-responsiveness' caveat still applies, just now demonstrated on a second model independently rather than assumed to be Qwen-specific.",
